<<<
 Here also a less undeniably wrong example may be of service: We have strong faith that a rally to the defence of the Act will be a feature of next year’s politics, if the Tory Party will have the courage to come into the open & declare war upon it. An assurance from the writer that by will have the courage he meant chooses to have the courage would be received, indeed, with incredulity, but would secure him a grudging acquittal; has the courage is what he should have written.
===
 Here also a less undeniably wrong example may be of service: *We have strong faith that a rally to the defence of the Act* will *be a feature of next year's politics, if the Tory Party* will *have the courage to come into the open & declare war upon it.* An assurance from the writer that by *will have the courage* he meant *chooses to have the courage* would be received, indeed, with incredulity, but would secure him a grudging acquittal; *has the courage* is what he should have written.
>>>

